<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <IsAotCompatible>false</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WireMock.Net lives here rather than in Verbara.Sdk.TestInfrastructure, and the split is
    load-bearing for coverage, not cosmetic.

    WireMock pulls ASP.NET Core dependencies, so any project referencing it acquires a
    FrameworkReference to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App. From that point the ~30 Microsoft.Extensions.*
    assemblies become framework-provided and stop being copied to the output directory. Coverlet's
    Mono.Cecil resolver only searches the module's own directory, so it then fails with
    CecilAssemblyResolutionException on Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions and SKIPS
    instrumenting every module that references it — silently, as a warning.

    Verbara.Sdk.TestInfrastructure is referenced by Verbara.Sdk.FunctionalTests and
    Verbara.Sdk.IntegrationTests, whose output carries Ami/Ari/Sessions/AudioSocket. Putting
    WireMock there cost those assemblies their instrumentation and dropped measured line coverage
    from 80.42% to 61.96% while every test still passed. Keeping it in a separate project that only
    the two HTTP provider suites reference confines both the FrameworkReference and the 156-package
    transitive graph to the suites that actually need them (ADR-0041 D7).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="WireMock.Net" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
